A *Harshad number* is a positive integer that is evenly divisible by the sum of its digits. For example, 18 is a Harshad number because its digit sum is $1+8=9$ and 18 is divisible by 9, while 19 is not (its digit sum is 10).

The class `DigitTools` contains the two methods below. You may not use `String` methods in either part.

```java
public class DigitTools {
    /** Returns the sum of the digits of n. Precondition: n >= 0 */
    public static int digitSum(int n)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Returns true if n > 0 and n is evenly divisible by digitSum(n); false otherwise. */
    public static boolean isHarshad(int n)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `digitSum`. For example, `digitSum(4083)` returns 15 and `digitSum(0)` returns 0.

**(b)** Write the method `isHarshad`, using `digitSum` as appropriate. For example, `isHarshad(18)` returns `true`, `isHarshad(19)` and `isHarshad(0)` return `false`.

sampleResponse:
```java
public static int digitSum(int n) {
    int sum = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        sum += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }
    return sum;
}

public static boolean isHarshad(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        return false;
    }
    return n % digitSum(n) == 0;
}
```
In `digitSum`, `n % 10` is the last digit and `n /= 10` drops it, so the loop visits every digit. In `isHarshad`, the `n <= 0` test must come first, because `digitSum(0)` is 0 and `n % 0` would throw an `ArithmeticException`.

